{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And they said: Is she to be a harlot, etc.</b> One needs to know what this response accomplishes regarding Jacob's concern over the destruction that had befallen him and his household. For even ab initio, if the nations demanded one of the community and designated her, they hand her over to them (Yerushalmi, Terumot 8:4) and do not all allow themselves to be killed on her account even for illicit relations — and all the more so in this case, where what had happened had already happened." "Or perhaps they intended to say that one should not be troubled over the matter, for it would only cause an uproar if they had killed without cause. But this killing was for a fitting reason: he took our sister against her will and made her a harlot — as they said: ״הַכְזוֹנָה יַעֲשֶׂה אֶת אֲחוֹתֵנוּ״. And the intent is not that he was liable for harlotry, for she was unmarried, and a son of Noah is commanded only regarding another's wife, and a male, and an animal, beast, and bird, and his sister from his mother." "<b>Or</b> it may intend to reply that on the contrary, there would have been danger among the nations when they saw that the humiliation of one man prevailed over the daughter of Jacob and acted and did as he pleased and desired — there would be no recourse for those who hate them among the peoples. On the contrary, through this their terror would be upon the peoples and they would tremble before them."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:06.593901248Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}